///1EDIT86
EDIT86 - CP/M-86 VT100/ANSI full-screen text editor

EDIT86 is an independent 8086 editor inspired by the supplied CB86 EDIT
2.04 package. It runs as a native CP/M-86 .CMD program. It requires no
CBASIC compiler, runtime library, DOS, PC BIOS or video memory.

The supplied EDIT.EXE is a DOS program, not a CP/M-86 executable.
EDIT86 replaces that platform-specific implementation. It is not a
complete source port: see DIFFERENCES before relying on old commands.

Start: EDIT86 TEST.TXT
Help:  HELP EDIT86
Inside the editor: press Escape, type HELP, press Return.

///2QUICKSTART
QUICK START

  A>EDIT86 TEST.TXT

An existing file is loaded. If no file can be opened under that name,
EDIT86 starts an empty buffer. If you omit the name, a filename prompt
appears. Use a CP/M name with at most eight name characters and three
extension characters, optionally preceded by a drive letter A: to P:.
Paths, spaces, wildcards and the temporary extension $$$ are refused.

Type text. Return splits a line; Backspace removes the character on the
left and joins lines when used at the start of a line. Editing always
inserts characters. Use arrows to move without changing text.

Save and continue: Ctrl-O, then Return to dismiss the result message.
Save and exit:     Escape, END, Return; Return after the save message.
Exit:              Ctrl-Q. If changed, type Y and Return to discard.

The status line shows filename, line, column and internal byte count.
An asterisk means the buffer has changed since the last successful save.

///2TERMINAL
TERMINAL SETTINGS

Use a VT100/ANSI terminal with at least 80 columns and 24 rows. EDIT86
uses the first 24 rows and does not query or resize the terminal.
Rows 1-22 show text; row 23 shows status; row 24 shows hints or prompts.
Long lines scroll horizontally. Long files scroll vertically.

The program uses ANSI cursor positioning, erase-to-end-of-line,
reverse video, and VT100 wrap mode. Cursor visibility uses the common
DEC extension ESC[?25h / ESC[?25l. Normal attributes, cursor visibility
and automatic wrapping are restored when the program exits normally.

Disable terminal software flow control (XON/XOFF), so Ctrl-S and Ctrl-Q
reach the editor. On a Mac terminal, use: stty -ixon
The Mac terminal must forward the intended arrow/Home/End/Page keys.

Arrow keys accept both CSI (ESC[) and SS3 (ESCO) forms. Home/End accept
H/F or 1~/4~/7~/8~. Page Up/Down use 5~/6~; Delete uses 3~.
Modified keys and mouse/paste protocols are not implemented. Disable
bracketed-paste mode or paste as ordinary text. Multiline paste with
LF characters moves the cursor; use CR line endings for inserted lines.

Escape alone opens the command prompt after a finite input-poll wait.
Its timing depends on emulator speed. Control-key movement is available
if a slow link separates bytes in an escape sequence.

///2KEYS
EDITING KEYS

Arrows        Move left, right, up, down.
Home / Ctrl-A Start of current line.
End           End of current line.
Page Up       Move up 21 lines.
Page Down     Move down 21 lines.
Ctrl-U        Move left.       Ctrl-F  Move right.
Ctrl-Z        Move up.         Ctrl-J  Move down.
Ctrl-T        Start of file.   Ctrl-B  End of file.
Ctrl-R        Move up 21 lines.
Return        Insert a line break.
Tab           Insert spaces to the next eight-column tab stop.
Backspace     Delete left; join with previous line at line start.
DEL byte 127  Same as Backspace.
Delete key    Delete right; join with next line on a line break.
Ctrl-N        Delete the entire current line, including its line break.
Ctrl-K        Copy current line to the clipboard, replacing its contents.
Ctrl-X        Copy current line to clipboard, then delete that line.
Ctrl-W        Insert clipboard contents at the current cursor position.
Ctrl-D        Clear clipboard.
Ctrl-G        Enter a search string.
Ctrl-S        Repeat the last search.
Ctrl-O        Save and continue editing.
Ctrl-Q        Quit; ask before discarding unsaved changes.
Escape        Open the command prompt.

Ctrl means hold the Control key while pressing the indicated letter.
Pressing Ctrl-Z edits/moves; it does not end text input.

///2COMMANDS
COMMAND PROMPT

Press Escape, type a command, and press Return. Commands ignore case.
Enter the command alone: leading/trailing spaces and arguments are not
accepted. Empty input or Escape returns to editing.

SAVE    Save the file and continue editing.
END     Save the file and exit if saving succeeds.
QUIT    Exit; ask for Y and Return when unsaved changes exist.
HELP    Display a compact key reference; any key returns to editing.
TOP     Move to the start of the file.
BOTTOM  Move to the end of the file.

A save result remains visible until Return is pressed. A failed END
returns to editing rather than discarding the buffer.

At input prompts, Backspace edits the response and Escape cancels.
No DOS command shell, directory commands or CP/M command execution are
available from this editor. Quit first to run another CP/M command.

///2SEARCH
SEARCH

Ctrl-G asks for literal text, up to 63 printable ASCII characters.
Return starts the search. Matching is case-sensitive and proceeds
forward from the character after the cursor, without wrapping.
Ctrl-S searches again with the same text. A match moves the cursor to
the first matching character; it does not highlight or replace text.

A failed search displays a message; press Return to continue.
An empty search string disables repeat search. Escape cancels the
prompt and also clears the previous search string.

Wildcards, regular expressions, search across line breaks and the old
EDIT ! pattern notation are not supported.

///2CLIPBOARD
LINE CLIPBOARD

Ctrl-K copies one complete line to a 2048-byte clipboard. The line's
newline is included if present. Ctrl-X copies and deletes that line.
Ctrl-W inserts the clipboard exactly at the cursor: use Home first to
insert copied whole lines before the current line.

Each copy/cut replaces the clipboard. This differs from the original
editor's accumulating 75-line buffer. A line longer than 2048 bytes
cannot be copied or cut; a message appears and the text is retained.
Ctrl-N can still delete such a line.

Clipboard contents survive saves and searches, but not program exit.
There is no undo, block selection, or multi-line accumulating clipboard.

///2FILES
TEXT FILE FORMAT AND LIMITS

The buffer holds at most 40000 bytes after input normalization.
ASCII characters 32-126 and LF are accepted. CR is discarded; tabs
expand to spaces at eight-column stops. Ctrl-Z marks end of text.
Other control characters and bytes above 126 cause loading to fail.
Binary files, UTF-8, accented text and CP437 artwork are not supported.
A rejected or oversized file is not opened for editing or rewritten.

Use CRLF or LF text files. CR-only files lose their line separators.
The buffer stores a line break as one LF. On saving, each LF becomes
CRLF. Disk size may therefore exceed the internal byte count.

Files use 128-byte CP/M records, padded with Ctrl-Z where needed.
A final newline is not added automatically. Saving expands tabs and
normalizes line endings even when the visible text was not changed.

Use a writable drive with space for the temporary copy and backup.
The editor uses the current CP/M user area. Drives are selected by an
optional filename prefix, for example B:NOTES.TXT.

///2SAVE
SAVING AND BACKUPS

EDIT86 writes NAME.$$$ first. If this temporary name already exists,
saving is refused; the existing temporary file is preserved.
After writing and closing the temporary file, EDIT86 renames the old
original to NAME.BAK and installs the new file under the original name.
For input NAME.BAK, the backup name is NAME.BK1 instead.

The previous backup of that name is replaced during a later save.
Files sharing the same base name also share temporary and backup names:
NOTE.TXT and NOTE.BAS both use NOTE.$$$ and NOTE.BAK.

A successful save clears the changed marker. This is not an undo or
version history facility. Keep separate copies of valuable files.

On a failed write/rename, do not erase recovery files blindly. Inspect
the original, NAME.$$$ and NAME.BAK (or .BK1) after exiting. The buffer
remains in memory after a failed save. It is not discarded by END.

If the original was already renamed to its backup but installing the
new file failed, the backup contains the old file and NAME.$$$ contains
the new text. Recover those files before starting another save.

///2DIFFERENCES
DIFFERENCES FROM CB86 EDIT 2.04

EDIT86 is an independent implementation, not a binary patch of EDIT.EXE
and not a rebuild of the supplied EDIT.BAS. No DOS runtime is included.
It uses native CP/M-86 BDOS calls and VT100/ANSI terminal output.

Common movement controls and line copy/cut/paste are retained, but the
clipboard is a single line rather than an accumulating 75-line buffer.
Ctrl-O saves; Ctrl-Q quits; Ctrl-S repeats a literal search. Ctrl-N
deletes the entire line. Character entry always inserts.

The original's macros, macro files, DOS shell commands, printer/spooler,
block operations, include/read/write range commands, pattern searches,
function-key character palette and attribute settings are not present.
Do not replay old EDIT macro files in this program.

The original DOS files and source remain unchanged.

///2EXAMPLE
SHORT EXERCISE

  A>EDIT86 NOTES.TXT

Type: First line
Press Return, then type: Second line
Press Ctrl-T to return to the start.
Press Ctrl-K to copy First line and its line break.
Press Ctrl-B to move to the end. Press Return, then Ctrl-W.
Press Ctrl-G, type Second, then Return to find that word.
Press Ctrl-O and Return to save.
Press Ctrl-Q to return to CP/M without a discard question.

Reopen NOTES.TXT to inspect the result. If a previous NOTES.TXT existed,
NOTES.BAK contains its contents from before that save.

///2BUILD
BUILD AND VALIDATION

The distributed EDIT86.CMD runs directly under CP/M-86. No compiler or
Python installation is required to use it.

For rebuilding on a development computer, BUILD.py requires Python 3
and NASM. Run: python3 BUILD.py EDIT86.CMD
The output is an 8086 single-group CP/M-86 command file, not a DOS EXE.

Automated tests execute the generated instructions in the RETRO86
project's tiny8086 core with a controlled BDOS/file-system fixture.
They exercise editing, ANSI keys, searching, saving, backups, temporary
file collisions and simulated I/O failures. This does not substitute
for a live terminal/ESP32 test, which has not been performed here.
